Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on Safe Fishing Operations of Fishing Vessels and Promotion of the Safety and Health of Fishing Vessel Crew Members and matters necessary for its enforcement. <Amended 2024.12.31>

Article 2 (Fishing Limit Line, etc.)
(1) "Line prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 2, subparagraph 3 of the Act on Safe Fishing Operations of Fishing Vessels and Promotion of the Safety and Health of Fishing Vessel Crew Members (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the line specified in attached Table 1. <Amended 2024.12.31>
(2) "Sea area within the scope prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 2, subparagraph 4 of the Act means the sea area specified in attached Table 2.
(3) "Line prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 2, subparagraph 5 of the Act means the line specified in attached Table 3.
(4) "Sea area within the scope prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 2, subparagraph 6 of the Act means the sea area specified in attached Table 4.

Article 3 (Exclusion from Application)
(1) Pursuant to the proviso to Article 3 of the Act, none of the provisions of the Act shall apply to the following fishing vessels: <Amended 2024.12.31>
1. Vessels engaged in fisheries testing, surveys, guidance, enforcement, or instruction under Article 2, subparagraph 1(b) of the Fishing Vessels Act;
2. Fishing vessels engaged in distant-water fisheries under Article 2, subparagraph 2 of the Distant Water Fisheries Development Act;
3. Fishing vessels engaged in inland-water fisheries under Article 2, subparagraph 5 of the Inland Water Fisheries Act;
4. Fishing vessels engaged in inland-water aquaculture under Article 10(1)7 of the Aquaculture Industry Development Act.
(2) Pursuant to the proviso to Article 3 of the Act, Chapters 4-2 and 6 of the Act (limited to penalties and administrative fines for violations related to Chapter 4-2 of the Act) shall not apply to fishing vessels that regularly employ fewer than five fishing vessel crew members, among fishing vessels other than those referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1). <Newly Inserted 2024.12.31>

Article 4 (Formulation, etc. of Master Plans on Fishing Vessel Safety, etc.)
(1) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to formulate or amend a master plan on fishing vessel safety, etc. under Article 7(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "master plan"), the Minister shall consult with the heads of the relevant administrative agencies. <Amended 2024.12.31>
(2) Where deemed necessary to formulate or amend a master plan, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may request the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies, Metropolitan City Mayors, Do Governors, or Special Self-Governing Province Governors (hereinafter referred to as "Mayors/Do Governors"), mayors, heads of Guns, or heads of Gus (the head of a Gu means the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply), or the heads of public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions (hereinafter referred to as "public institutions") to submit materials, present opinions, or provide other necessary cooperation.
(3) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries has formulated or amended a master plan, the Minister shall notify the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies, Mayors/Do Governors, mayors, heads of Guns, heads of Gus, and heads of public institutions of its contents.

Article 5 (Formulation, etc. of Implementation Plans on Fishing Vessel Safety, etc.)
(1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall formulate and implement an annual implementation plan on fishing vessel safety, etc. (hereinafter referred to as an "implementation plan") to implement the master plan. <Amended 2024.12.31>
(2) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to formulate an implementation plan, the Minister shall prepare guidelines for the formulation of implementation plans on fishing vessel safety, etc. for individual institutions (hereinafter referred to as "institutional implementation plans" in this Article) and notify the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies, Mayors/Do Governors, mayors, heads of Guns, heads of Gus, and heads of public institutions (hereinafter referred to as "authorities responsible for preparation at each institution" in this Article) of the guidelines by August 31 each year. <Amended 2024.12.31>
(3) Upon receiving notification of the formulation guidelines pursuant to paragraph (2), the authorities responsible for preparation at each institution shall prepare their institutional implementation plans for the following year and submit them to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries by October 31 each year. <Amended 2024.12.31>
(4) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems that an institutional implementation plan submitted pursuant to paragraph (3) is contrary to the master plan or needs to be supplemented, the Minister may request revision or supplementation of the relevant institutional implementation plan.
(5) Where deemed necessary for the formulation of an implementation plan, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may request the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies, Mayors/Do Governors, mayors, heads of Guns, heads of Gus, or heads of public institutions to submit materials, present opinions, or provide other necessary cooperation.
(6) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall consolidate and coordinate the institutional implementation plans submitted pursuant to paragraphs (3) and (4), finalize the implementation plan, and notify the authorities responsible for preparation at each institution thereof.
(7) The authorities responsible for preparation at each institution shall submit the results of implementation of their institutional implementation plans for the preceding year to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries by the last day of February each year.

Article 6 (Exceptions to the Prohibition on Crossing the Fishing Limit Line or Fishing Restraint Line)
"Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as fishing vessels in areas near the fishing limit line or fishing restraint line" in the proviso to Article 11 of the Act means the following cases: <Amended 2023.11.7>
1. Where fishing vessels from areas or islands near the fishing limit line or fishing restraint line engage in fishing operations or navigate in sea areas prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, in compliance with the periods and fishing operation conditions prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries;
2. Where fishing vessels approved by the Minister of Unification pursuant to Article 20(1) of the Inter-Korean Exchange and Cooperation Act, or fishing vessels heading out to fish in the relevant foreign waters after obtaining fishing permits from the foreign government, navigate along routes prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries;
3. Where a fishing vessel for which a port or harbor located north of the fishing limit line has been designated as its port of registry when registering the fishing vessel pursuant to Article 13 of the Fishing Vessels Act departs from or enters that port or harbor (hereinafter referred to as "port departure/entry").

Article 7 (Period of Validity of Fishing Departure Registration)
The period of validity of fishing departure registration under the latter part of Article 12(1) of the Act shall be one year from the date of registration.

Article 8 (Methods and Procedures for Temporary Restrictions on Fishing Operations or Navigation)
(1) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or a Mayor/Do Governor temporarily restricts fishing operations or navigation pursuant to Article 16(2) of the Act, the Minister or Mayor/Do Governor shall post the following matters on the website of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries or the relevant City/Do and notify fishing vessels engaging in fishing operations or navigating in the relevant sea area of the following matters using wireless equipment, fax, or other means. In such cases, where notification to fishing vessels has been entrusted to the National Federation of Fisheries Cooperatives (hereinafter referred to as the "National Federation") pursuant to Article 19(2) of the Act, the National Federation or the Fishing Vessel Safe Operations Headquarters under paragraph (3) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as the "Safety Headquarters") shall provide the notification.
1. The purpose of the restrictions on fishing operations or navigation;
2. The times, periods, and scope of sea areas of the restrictions on fishing operations or navigation;
3. Other matters necessary for the safety of fishing vessels.
(2) When the need for temporary restrictions on fishing operations or navigation no longer exists, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or a Mayor/Do Governor shall, in consultation with the Minister of National Defense or the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard, lift the restrictions on fishing operations or navigation without delay and post the lifting of the restrictions on the website of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries or the relevant City/Do.

Article 9 (Fishing Grounds in Border Sea Areas of the West Sea)
"Fishing grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 17(1) of the Act means fishing grounds surrounding the following islands:
1. Baengnyeongdo;
2. Daecheongdo;
3. Socheongdo;
4. Yeonpyeongdo;
5. Ganghwado.

Article 10 (Control, etc. of Port Departure or Entry at Fishing Grounds in Border Sea Areas of the West Sea)
(1) Where the commander of the military unit having jurisdiction needs to control port departure/entry by fishing vessels at fishing grounds in border sea areas of the West Sea under Article 9 for national security or the conduct of military operations pursuant to Article 17(1) of the Act, the commander shall notify the head of the reporting agency of that fact, and the head of the reporting agency receiving the notification shall cooperate in controlling port departure/entry at the port or harbor where the reporting agency is located. <Amended 2023.11.7>
(2) Where the commander of the military unit having jurisdiction needs to provide guidance on safe fishing operations by fishing vessels at fishing grounds in border sea areas of the West Sea under Article 9 for national security or the conduct of military operations, the commander may request cooperation in related matters from the head of the Fisheries Management Service having jurisdiction and the mayor, head of the Gun, or head of the Gu, and the heads of the agencies receiving the request shall comply therewith.

Article 11 (Establishment and Operation of Fishing Operations Protection Headquarters)
(1) Pursuant to Article 18(1) of the Act, the East Sea Fishing Operations Protection Headquarters and the West Sea Fishing Operations Protection Headquarters shall be established at the Sokcho Coast Guard Station and the Incheon Coast Guard Station, respectively.
(2) The head of a Fishing Operations Protection Headquarters under paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as the "head of a Fishing Operations Protection Headquarters") shall be the head of the coast guard station with jurisdiction, and its members shall be persons designated by the head of the Fishing Operations Protection Headquarters from among police officials belonging to the coast guard station with jurisdiction.
(3) The head of a Fishing Operations Protection Headquarters may establish and operate a Fishing Operations Protection Council for cooperation among relevant agencies in maintaining fishing order and fishing safety in specific sea areas, including preventing fishing vessels operating in specific sea areas from leaving their fishing grounds and preventing seizure or hijacking.
(4) Matters necessary for the composition, functions, and operation of a Fishing Operations Protection Council under paragraph (3) shall be determined by the head of the Fishing Operations Protection Headquarters in consultation with the heads of relevant agencies, such as the head of the Fisheries Management Service with jurisdiction, the Mayor, head of a Gun, or head of a Gu, the commander of a military unit, and the head of the Safety Headquarters.

Article 12 (Frequency and Procedures for Position Notifications, Etc.)
(1) A position notification under Article 21(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "position notification") shall be made by notifying the Safety Headquarters specified in the following classifications of the latitude and longitude: Provided, That, where communication with the Safety Headquarters specified in each classification is impossible, the position notification shall be made to a nearby Safety Headquarters.
1. When proceeding to a specific sea area to fish: The Safety Headquarters with jurisdiction over the relevant specific sea area;
2. In cases other than those under subparagraph 1: The Safety Headquarters with jurisdiction over the port of departure.
(2) The frequency of position notifications shall be as specified in the following classifications: <Amended on October 8, 2024>
1. Fishing vessels proceeding to a specific sea area or fishing restraint sea area to fish: Position notifications shall be made as follows:
(a) A position notification shall be made when entering a specific sea area or fishing restraint sea area;
(b) A position notification shall be made within 12 hours of the time of the notification under item (a) or the time of departure for a specific sea area, and each subsequent notification shall be made within 12 hours of the time of the immediately preceding notification;
(c) A position notification shall be made when leaving a specific sea area or fishing restraint sea area;
2. Fishing vessels proceeding to a general sea area to fish: A position notification shall be made within 24 hours of the time of departure for a general sea area or the time of entry into a general sea area, and each subsequent notification shall be made within 24 hours of the time of the immediately preceding notification.
(3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2)1(b) and subparagraph 2 of that paragraph, where a rough sea alert or typhoon alert takes effect, position notifications shall be made as specified in the following classifications: <Amended on October 8, 2024>
1. Where a rough sea alert takes effect in a general sea area: A position notification shall be made within 12 hours of the time specified in the following classifications, and each subsequent notification shall be made within 12 hours of the time of the immediately preceding notification: Provided, That, in the case of a fishing vessel for which, as of the time under item (a) or (b)1), less than 12 hours remain until the time by which a notification must be made pursuant to paragraph (2)2, a position notification shall be made within that remaining time, and each subsequent notification shall be made within 12 hours of the time of the immediately preceding notification.
(a) Where a vessel is fishing or navigating in the general sea area affected by a rough sea alert at the time the alert takes effect: The time the relevant rough sea alert takes effect;
(b) Where a vessel enters a general sea area in which a rough sea alert is in effect from any of the following sea areas: The time of entry into the general sea area in which the relevant rough sea alert is in effect:
    1) A general sea area in which neither a rough sea alert nor a typhoon alert is in effect;
    2) A general sea area in which a typhoon alert is in effect;
    3) A specific sea area or fishing restraint sea area;
(c) Where a vessel departs for a general sea area in which a rough sea alert (limited to a rough sea advisory) is in effect: The time of departure for the relevant general sea area;
2. Where a typhoon alert takes effect in a specific sea area, fishing restraint sea area, or general sea area, and a vessel is fishing or navigating in the affected specific sea area, fishing restraint sea area, or general sea area at the time the typhoon alert takes effect: A position notification shall be made within 4 hours of the time the relevant typhoon alert takes effect, and each subsequent notification shall be made within 4 hours of the time of the immediately preceding notification: Provided, That, in the case of a fishing vessel for which, as of the time the relevant typhoon alert takes effect, less than 4 hours remain until the time by which a notification must be made pursuant to paragraph (2)1(b) or subparagraph 2 of that paragraph, a position notification shall be made within that remaining time, and each subsequent notification shall be made within 4 hours of the time of the immediately preceding notification.
(4) Where a rough sea alert or typhoon alert under paragraph (3) is lifted, position notifications shall be made pursuant to paragraph (2)1(b) or subparagraph 2 of that paragraph. In such cases, where a position notification was made pursuant to paragraph (3) before the relevant rough sea alert or typhoon alert was lifted, position notifications shall be made by deeming the time of the last such notification to be the time of the immediately preceding notification under paragraph (2)1(b) or subparagraph 2 of that paragraph. <Added on October 8, 2024>

Article 13 (Methods of Confirming Positions, Etc.)
(1) The position of a fishing vessel under Article 21(3) of the Act shall be confirmed using broadcasts through wireless communications, the Comprehensive Fishing Vessel Safety Management System under Article 19(1)8 of the Act, and other means.
(2) The notification under Article 21(3) of the Act shall be made by notifying the coast guard station and Fisheries Management Service with jurisdiction of fishing vessels that have failed to make a position notification within the time prescribed by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 13-2 (Duties of Fishing Vessel Supervisors)
The term "duties prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 27 of the Act means the following duties:
1. Conducting safety and health inspections of machinery, apparatus, or facilities related to work directed and supervised aboard a fishing vessel by a fishing vessel supervisor under Article 27 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "fishing vessel supervisor"), and checking for abnormalities;
2. Inspecting the work clothing, protective gear, and protective devices of fishing vessel crew members, and providing training and guidance on their wearing and use;
3. Reporting fishing vessel crew accidents occurring in work directed and supervised by the fishing vessel supervisor, and taking emergency measures in response;
4. Checking and supervising the tidying and organization of workplaces and the securing of passageways for work directed and supervised by the fishing vessel supervisor;
5. The following duties concerning risk assessments conducted pursuant to Article 28 of the Act:
(a) Participating in the identification of harmful and hazardous factors;
(b) Participating in the implementation of improvement measures;
6. Other duties that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary and publicly notifies for maintaining and improving safety and health in work directed and supervised by the fishing vessel supervisor.

Article 13-3 (Establishment and Operation of an Integrated Information System for the Prevention of Fishing Vessel Crew Accidents, Etc.)
(1) The information to be covered by the establishment and operation of the integrated information system for the prevention of fishing vessel crew accidents under Article 30(1) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Information on fishing vessels subject to coverage under Article 6 of the Act on Accident Compensation Insurance for Fishing Vessel Crew Members and Fishing Vessels;
2. Information on the occurrence of fishing vessel crew accidents;
3. Information on the safety and health of fishing vessel crew members;
4. Other information that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary and publicly notifies for the prevention of fishing vessel crew accidents.
(2) Matters necessary for the establishment and operation, etc. of the integrated information system for the prevention of fishing vessel crew accidents, other than those provided for in paragraph (1), shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 13-4 (Vessels for Which Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Improvement Plans Are to Be Prepared)
The term "fishing vessels on which the number of persons who have developed occupational diseases is at least the number prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 34(1)3 of the Act means fishing vessels on which 2 or more persons develop occupational diseases in a year.

Article 13-5 (Fishing Vessels Required to Establish Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committees, etc.)
(1) The fishing vessels for which a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee under Article 36 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee") must be established and operated shall be as follows:
1. A fishing vessel on which fishing vessel crew accidents have occurred at least twice within the past year;
2. A fishing vessel on which a serious fishing vessel crew accident has occurred.
(2) A fishing vessel owner shall establish a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee within one month from the date on which the fishing vessel falls under any subparagraph of paragraph (1).
(3) The operating period of a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee shall be one year from the date on which it is established pursuant to paragraph (2): Provided, That where the fishing vessel again falls under any subparagraph of paragraph (1) during that operating period, the period shall run anew for one year from the date on which the fishing vessel falls under the relevant subparagraph.

Article 13-6 (Matters Subject to Deliberation and Resolution by Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committees)
The following matters shall be subject to deliberation and resolution by the Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee:
1. Matters concerning risk assessments under Article 28 (1) of the Act;
2. Matters concerning the formulation of a fishing vessel safety and health improvement plan under Article 34 (1) of the Act;
3. Other important matters concerning the safety and health of fishing vessel crew members that are determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 13-7 (Composition and Operation of Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committees, etc.)
(1) A Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee shall consist of no more than eight members, including a chairperson.
(2) The members of a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee shall be the persons in the following categories. In such cases, the number of members falling under subparagraph 1 shall be equal to the number of members falling under subparagraph 2.
1. The persons falling under the following items:
(a) The fishing vessel owner;
(b) The fishing vessel supervisor;
(c) No more than two fishing vessel crew members designated by the fishing vessel owner.
2. The persons falling under the following items:
(a) The fishing vessel crew representative (meaning a fishing vessel crew member elected by and from among the fishing vessel crew members other than those falling under subparagraph 1 (c); hereinafter the same shall apply);
(b) No more than three fishing vessel crew members designated by the fishing vessel crew representative from among the fishing vessel crew members other than those falling under subparagraph 1 (c).
(3) The chairperson of a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "chairperson") shall be elected by and from among its members. In such cases, one member falling under paragraph (2) 1 and one member falling under paragraph (2) 2 may be elected as co-chairpersons.
(4) Meetings of a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee shall be classified as regular meetings and extraordinary meetings.
(5) Regular meetings of a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee shall be convened quarterly by the chairperson (meaning the co-chairpersons where co-chairpersons have been elected pursuant to the latter part of paragraph (3); hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article), and extraordinary meetings shall be convened when the chairperson deems it necessary.
(6) A meeting of a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee shall open with the attendance of a majority of the members falling under each of paragraph (2) 1 and 2, and resolutions shall be adopted with the affirmative votes of a majority of the members present.
(7) A Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee shall prepare and retain minutes recording the following matters:
1. The date, time, and place of the meeting;
2. The members present;
3. The matters deliberated and the resolutions adopted.
(8) The chairperson shall promptly inform fishing vessel crew members of the results of meetings, including the matters deliberated and resolved by the Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee, by appropriate means such as posting notices on board the vessel.
(9) Matters necessary for the composition and operation of a Fishing Vessel Safety and Health Committee, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (8), shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 13-8 (Fishing Vessel Crew Safety Inspectors)
(1) A fishing vessel crew safety inspector under Article 44 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "fishing vessel crew safety inspector") shall be a public official of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries or an agency under its jurisdiction who falls under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. A person who, as a Grade 5 or higher public official, has at least six months of experience working at the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries or an agency under its jurisdiction;
2. A person who, as a Grade 6 or Grade 7 public official, has at least two years of experience working at an oceans and fisheries authority (meaning the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, a Fisheries Management Service, a Regional Office of Oceans and Fisheries, or an Oceans and Fisheries Office; hereinafter the same shall apply);
3. A person who, as a Grade 6 or Grade 7 public official, has at least one year of experience handling affairs concerning fishing vessel crew members at an oceans and fisheries authority;
4. A Grade 6 or Grade 7 public official who has at least two years of experience handling affairs concerning fishing vessel crew members at an oceans and fisheries authority (including experience before becoming a Grade 6 or Grade 7 public official);
5. A Grade 6 or Grade 7 public official who has at least five years of experience working in the fisheries field at an oceans and fisheries authority (including experience before becoming a Grade 6 or Grade 7 public official).
(2) Fishing vessel crew safety inspectors assigned to the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries shall be appointed and removed by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, and fishing vessel crew safety inspectors assigned to agencies under the jurisdiction of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries shall be appointed and removed by the heads of the respective agencies.
(3) Fishing vessel crew safety inspectors shall receive reports of violations of the Act or orders issued under the Act pursuant to Article 47 (1) of the Act, and perform other duties determined by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries as necessary for supervising the safety and health of fishing vessel crew members.

Article 14 (Financial Support)
The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the head of a local government may implement the following financial support projects pursuant to Article 48 (1) of the Act. <Amended 2024.12.31>
1. Support for the development of technologies to prevent and respond to fishing vessel safety accidents;
2. Support for the provision of fishing vessel safety equipment and installations;
3. Other support deemed necessary by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the head of a local government to prevent and respond to fishing vessel safety accidents.

Article 15 (Delegation of Authority)
(1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall delegate the following authority to the head of the Fisheries Management Service pursuant to Article 51 of the Act: <Amended on December 31, 2024>
1. Orders or measures concerning stopping vessels, boarding inspections, etc. under Article 23 of the Act;
2. Requests for revocation or suspension of fishing permits, etc. under Article 49(1) of the Act.
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall delegate the following authority to the head of a Regional Office of Oceans and Fisheries pursuant to Article 51 of the Act: <Newly inserted on December 31, 2024>
1. Orders to formulate and implement fishing vessel safety and health improvement plans under Article 34(1) of the Act;
2. Receipt of fishing vessel safety and health improvement plans under Article 35(1) of the Act, and examination of, notification concerning, and orders to supplement fishing vessel safety and health improvement plans under paragraph (2) of that Article;
3. Orders for corrective measures under Article 39(1) of the Act, orders to suspend work under paragraph (3) of that Article, receipt of requests to lift suspensions of use or work under paragraph (4) of that Article, and lifting of suspensions of use or work under paragraph (5) of that Article;
4. Receipt of reports on the occurrence of serious fishing vessel crew accidents under Article 40(2) of the Act;
5. Orders to suspend fishing operations or navigation under Article 41(1) of the Act, orders to suspend fishing operations or navigation and evacuate fishing vessel crew members under paragraph (2) of that Article, and lifting of suspensions of fishing operations or navigation under paragraph (3) of that Article;
6. Investigation into the causes of serious fishing vessel crew accidents under Article 42(1) of the Act;
7. Receipt of reports on the outline, causes, etc. of fishing vessel crew accidents under Article 43(3) of the Act;
8. Imposition and collection of administrative fines under Article 58(1) through (3) of the Act, subparagraphs 4 through 8 of paragraph (4) of that Article, subparagraph 6 of paragraph (5) of that Article, and paragraph (6) of that Article.
(3) The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard shall delegate the following authority to the heads of coast guard stations pursuant to Article 51 of the Act: <Amended on December 31, 2024>
1. Orders or measures concerning stopping vessels, boarding inspections, etc. under Article 23 of the Act;
2. Imposition and collection of administrative fines under Article 58(4)3 of the Act and subparagraphs 1, 2, 4, and 5 of paragraph (5) of that Article.

Article 16 (Processing of Sensitive Information and Personally Identifiable Information)
(1) Where unavoidable for performing the following affairs, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries (including a person to whom the relevant authority has been delegated or entrusted) may process materials containing health information under Article 23 of the Personal Information Protection Act (limited to the performance of affairs under subparagraphs 2 and 3) and resident registration numbers, passport numbers, or alien registration numbers under subparagraphs 1, 2, or 4 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of that Act: <Amended on December 31, 2024>
1. Affairs concerning the operation and management of the comprehensive fishing vessel safety management system under Article 19(1)8 of the Act;
2. Affairs concerning the establishment and operation of the integrated information system for preventing fishing vessel crew accidents under Article 30 of the Act;
3. Affairs concerning receipt of reports on the outline, causes, etc. of fishing vessel crew accidents under Article 43(3) of the Act.
(2) Where unavoidable for performing duties concerning port departure and entry reports under Article 8 of the Act, the head of a reporting agency may process materials containing resident registration numbers, passport numbers, or alien registration numbers under subparagraphs 1, 2, or 4 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act. <Amended on December 31, 2024>

Article 17 (Criteria for Imposition of Administrative Fines)
The criteria for imposing administrative fines under Article 58(1) through (6) of the Act shall be as specified in Annex 5. <Amended on December 31, 2024>

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 30976, August 26, 2020>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on August 28, 2020.

Article 2 (Transitional Measures concerning the Validity Period of Fishing Departure Registration)
A fishing departure registration made before this Decree enters into force with an agency responsible for reporting and controlling the departure or entry of fishing vessels shall be deemed a fishing departure registration made pursuant to Article 12 of the Act, and the validity period under Article 7 shall apply from the date of that registration.

ADDENDA (Presidential Decree Partially Amending 61 Statutes to Support the Economic Recovery of Micro Enterprises) <Presidential Decree No. 33434, April 25, 2023>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on the date of its promulgation.

Article 2 (Applicability to Administrative Dispositions, Penalty Surcharges, or Administrative Fines)
The amended provisions of Articles 1 through 61 shall also apply where an administrative disposition is made or a penalty surcharge or administrative fine is imposed after this Decree enters into force for a violation committed before this Decree enters into force.

ADDENDUM <Presidential Decree No. 33851, November 7, 2023>
This Decree shall enter into force on the date of its promulgation: Provided, That the amended provisions of items (g) through (i) of subparagraph 2 of Annex 5 shall enter into force on October 19, 2025.

ADDENDA (Enforcement Decree of the Special Act on the Establishment of Gangwon Special Self-Governing Province and Development of a Global City for Future Industries) <Presidential Decree No. 34550, June 4, 2024>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on June 8, 2024.

Articles 2 through 4 Omitted.

Article 5 (Amendments to Other Statutes)
(1) through (19) Omitted.
(20) The Enforcement Decree of the Fishing Vessel Safe Operations Act is partially amended as follows:
In subparagraph 1(a) of Annex 1, "Gangwon-do" shall be replaced by "Gangwon Special Self-Governing Province."
(21) through (44) Omitted.

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 34939, October 8, 2024>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on the date of its promulgation.

Article 2 (Transitional Measures concerning the Frequency, etc. of Position Notifications)
The previous provisions of Article 12(2) and (3) shall apply to the frequency, etc. of position notifications by fishing vessels that departed before this Decree enters into force until those fishing vessels enter port, notwithstanding the amended provisions of Article 12(2) through (4).

ADDENDA <Presidential Decree No. 35168, December 31, 2024>

Article 1 (Enforcement Date)
This Decree shall enter into force on January 3, 2025: Provided, That the amended provisions of subparagraph 2(h) of Annex 5 shall enter into force on October 19, 2025.

Article 2 (Special Cases concerning Criteria for Imposition of Administrative Fines)
(1) From January 3, 2025, through October 18, 2025, "in violation of Article 24(1) of the Act, a life jacket or life-saving garment" in the amended provisions of the violation column in subparagraph 2(g) of Annex 5 shall be construed as "in violation of Article 24 of the Act, a life jacket."
(2) From January 3, 2025, through October 18, 2025, "Article 58(5)6 of the Act" in the amended provisions of the column for the applicable statutory provisions in subparagraph 2(i) of Annex 5 shall be construed as "Article 58(5)5 of the Act."
(3) From January 3, 2025, through October 18, 2025, "Article 58(5)7 of the Act" in the amended provisions of the column for the applicable statutory provisions in subparagraph 2(l) of Annex 5 shall be construed as "Article 58(5)6 of the Act."

Article 3 (Amendments to Other Statutes)
(1) The Enforcement Decree of the Act on the Operation of Direct Payment Programs to Promote Public Interest Functions of Fisheries and Fishing Villages is partially amended as follows:
Subparagraph 16 of Annex 1-2 shall be amended as follows:
16. Act on Safe Fishing Operations of Fishing Vessels and Promotion of the Safety and Health of Fishing Vessel Crew Members.
